Build me a Mapillary facade harvester in Python. It discovers, filters, and
downloads 360° street-level imagery of Barcelona, then reprojects it into
rectified straight-on facade crops I can turn into game textures.

Context: I'm building a night driving game in Godot set in Barcelona. Building
footprints already exist from the Spanish Cadastre (INSPIRE buildingpart GML,
EPSG:25831) — read ./out/manifest.json for the world origin and CRS, and
./work/ for the footprint data. Don't assume paths, look first.

Write the stage skeleton, the config schema, and the on-disk layout FIRST.
Show me that and wait for approval before implementing reprojection.

═══ CREDENTIALS — DO THIS RIGHT ═══
Read MAPILLARY_CLIENT_TOKEN from the environment, loaded from a .env file via
python-dotenv. Add .env to .gitignore as your first action if it isn't there.
Never hardcode it, never log it, never put it in a committed config file, and
strip it from any URL you write to logs or the manifest.
This tool needs ONLY the client token. It does not need the client secret or
the auth URL — those are for the OAuth user-token flow, which read-only access
to public imagery does not use. If you think you need them, stop and tell me
why instead of adding them.
Auth: client token as ?access_token= for tiles.mapillary.com, and as an
"Authorization: OAuth <token>" header for graph.mapillary.com.

═══ STAGE 1 — DISCOVER (vector tiles) ═══
Do NOT use graph.mapillary.com/images?bbox= for discovery. Its limit caps at
2000 with no pagination outside creator_username queries, so in a dense city it
silently truncates. Use vector tiles.

Endpoint: https://tiles.mapillary.com/maps/vtp/mly1_computed_public/2/14/{x}/{y}
Use the COMPUTED endpoint, not mly1_public — raw GPS in Eixample street canyons
is off by 10-20m, enough to attribute a photo to the wrong building. Computed
geometries are SfM-corrected.

Enumerate z14 tiles covering my bbox (default 1.95,41.28,2.30,41.52, override
via CLI). Decode MVT with mapbox-vector-tile. From the "image" layer at z14
collect: id, geometry, captured_at, compass_angle, is_pano, sequence_id,
quality_score.

Cache every tile response to ./work/mapillary/tiles/ and skip on re-run. Tile
requests are limited to 50,000/day — I should never hit that, but caching means
a crashed run doesn't re-spend the budget.

Write ./work/mapillary/candidates.parquet. Print: tile count, total images,
pano count, date range.

═══ STAGE 2 — FILTER ═══
Apply in this order, logging how many survive each step:

1. is_pano == true. HARD REQUIREMENT, drop everything else.
   Reason, so you don't "optimise" this away: perspective dashcam images face
   down the street, putting facades at 70-80 degrees obliquity — they rectify
   into a smeared mess. Equirectangular panos let me reproject a virtual camera
   perpendicular to the facade. Non-pano images are worthless for this task.
2. quality_score > 0.7 (configurable; null scores are dropped, not kept).
3. captured_at within the last N years (default 6).
4. Local time-of-day from captured_at in Europe/Madrid: reject before 10:00 and
   after 17:00 local. Raking low-angle light bakes hard shadows into the albedo
   and I can't remove those later.
5. Dedupe within sequence_id: one drive yields hundreds of near-identical
   frames. Keep at most one image per sequence per 25 m of travel.

═══ STAGE 3 — MATCH TO FACADES ═══
Load the Cadastre building footprints. For each footprint, extract edges that
front a street (test against the OSM road geometry already in ./work/).

For each street-facing edge:
  - midpoint M, outward normal N
  - find candidate panos within 8-30 m of M, on the outward side of the edge
  - score by: distance to the ideal standoff (default 18 m), angle between
    (camera -> M) and -N (want near 0, i.e. camera square to the facade), and
    quality_score
  - keep the top K (default 2)

Reproject everything into EPSG:25831 for these distance calculations. Do NOT do
geometry in degrees.

Skip facades shorter than 4 m and any facade with no candidate — log both counts.

═══ STAGE 4 — FETCH METADATA ═══
Only for selected images. Batch via https://graph.mapillary.com?ids=ID1,ID2,...
fields: id,thumb_2048_url,computed_geometry,computed_compass_angle,
computed_rotation,camera_type,captured_at,creator,width,height

Entity API allows 60,000 req/min so rate limits aren't the constraint, but add
polite concurrency (default 8 workers) with exponential backoff on the
"Application request limit reached" error (code 4).

Treat thumb_2048_url as short-lived signed CDN links. Resolve and download in
the same run — never persist a URL for a later session.

═══ STAGE 5 — DOWNLOAD ═══
*** Before downloading anything, print a plan: image count, estimated bytes    ***
*** (assume 600 KB/image), and exit if --dry-run. I want to approve the volume ***
*** before it starts. Expected range is 1.5-3 GB; if your plan says 200 GB,    ***
*** the filters are broken — say so instead of downloading.                    ***

Save to ./work/mapillary/raw/{image_id}.jpg. Resumable: skip existing files,
verify size, retry partial downloads. This job must survive being killed.

═══ STAGE 6 — REPROJECT (the actual deliverable) ═══
For each (facade, pano) pair, extract a rectified perspective view:

  - Treat the 2048x1024 equirectangular image as a sphere.
  - Level it using computed_rotation (axis-angle, OpenSfM convention) so the
    horizon is flat — skipping this leaves every facade tilted.
  - Virtual camera yaw = bearing from computed_geometry to facade midpoint,
    referenced to computed_compass_angle.
  - Pitch upward so the facade is vertically centred: derive from the standoff
    distance and the building height (I have heights from the Cadastre
    numberOfFloorsAboveGround — use them, don't guess).
  - Horizontal FOV to cover the facade width plus 10% margin, clamped to 90
    degrees; wider than that and the edges distort badly.
  - Output ./out/facades/{building_id}_{edge_index}_{image_id}.png

Write ./out/facades/contact_sheet_{tile}.jpg — a grid of thumbnails per tile so
I can eyeball a hundred results at once instead of opening files one by one.

═══ STAGE 7 — MANIFEST ═══
./out/facades/manifest.json, one record per output crop:
  output filename, mapillary image_id, creator username, captured_at,
  cadastre building reference, edge index, standoff distance, incidence angle,
  quality_score, reprojection parameters used.

Also write ./out/facades/ATTRIBUTION.md: the CC BY-SA 4.0 credit block listing
every contributing username with links. Mapillary imagery is CC BY-SA, which is
share-alike — the manifest is how I later identify which textures are
licence-encumbered if I want to replace them with owned art.

═══ REQUIREMENTS ═══
- Single script, argparse, pinned requirements.txt.
- --tiles X,Y to run exactly one z14 tile. I will test on one Eixample tile
  before running the city.
- --dry-run at every stage, not just download.
- Every stage writes its output to disk and skips if already present. I want to
  re-run stage 6 with different reprojection params without re-downloading.
- Structured progress with counts and timings per stage.
- Log every rejected candidate with image_id and the specific filter that killed
  it, to ./work/mapillary/rejected.csv. When the yield is bad I need to know
  which filter is too tight.

═══ FIRST TEST, DO THIS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE ═══
Run one z14 tile over the Eixample end to end and show me the contact sheet.
If the crops are looking down the street rather than at buildings, the bearing
or the compass reference is wrong. If they're tilted, computed_rotation isn't
being applied. Twenty images will tell us both in thirty seconds.